    "id": "q15",
    "view": {
      "mode": "multiple",
      "stem": "Compare evaluating $\\sqrt2\\,\\sqrt3$ by obtaining the roots separately and multiplying, with simplifying the exact product before lookup. In the separate-root calculation, $\\sqrt3=\\sqrt{75}/5$ may be used with the supplied cells. Select every true statement.\n\nTable IV (Square roots), literal excerpt from V. M. Bradis, *Four-figure Mathematical Tables*, Drofa, Moscow, 2010, 13th stereotype edition, printed pages 35, 36, 38. Decimal commas are shown as decimal points.\nFor a row below 10, main column $c$ refers to $N+0.01c$; for a row at least 10, it refers to $N+0.1c$. A correction column refers to the fourth significant argument digit, and its integer is in units of $0.001$ of the root.\n\n| Row N | Main column : entry | Correction column : integer | Next main column : entry |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 2.0 | 0 : 1.414 | - | - |\n| 75 | 0 : 8.660 | - | - |\n| 6.0 | 0 : 2.449 | - | - |",
      "figure_ids": [],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "The separate-root table calculation gives $1.414(8.660/5)=2.449048$.",
          "figure_ids": []
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "The exact product is $\\sqrt5$, so one lookup at $5$ replaces the two roots.",
          "figure_ids": []
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Simplifying to $\\sqrt6$ gives the table number $2.449$ and avoids multiplying two separately rounded roots.",
          "figure_ids": []
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "The separate-root table product is exactly $2.449$.",
          "figure_ids": []
        }
      ]
    }
  },
